# Match the spirit of .gitignore but tighter — anything that should
# never reach the Docker build context. Reduces context-upload size and
# stops local-only secrets (.keys/private.pem) from ever landing in an
# image layer even if Docker is run with a permissive UID.

# Local secrets — NEVER ship in any image.
.keys/
*.pem
!keys/public.pem

# Local bootstrap marker — image runs bootstrap fresh.
.bootstrap-complete

# VCS metadata is EXCLUDED, by the `.*` rule below and with no negation. A build
# context is uploaded whole — to a remote or shared builder in many setups — and
# .git carries config (origin URLs sometimes hold credentials), reflogs and every
# historical object. `COPY . .` then persists all of it in an image layer and the
# build cache, where no later cleanup step can reach it. docker/README.md states
# this to contributors as a guarantee about their local history.
#
# The cost is real and is accepted: gates that ask git which files are tracked,
# which are ignored, or what changed against the base cannot answer in this
# image, so they skip and say so. A skipped gate here is not a passing gate —
# run the suite outside Docker, or read CI, for those.

# Node + tool state.
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# Build / runtime artifacts.
dist/
build/
out/
findings.json
scan-output.json
*.tmp
*.bak
*.log

# OS / editor noise.
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.suo
*.ntvs*
*.njsproj
*.sln

# Dotfiles and local state — catch-all first, then re-admit repo content. This
# mirrors .gitignore's own structure, and for the same reason: naming individual
# tools here would bake one contributor's setup into a shared file, and the list
# would go stale the moment anyone adopted a different one.
#
# Docker has no user-level ignore file — only this path is consulted — so the
# catch-all is the only way to keep session and agent state out of the build
# context and the image layers without enumerating it. Anything excluded through
# a contributor's own global gitignore is covered here by shape, not by name.
#
# The negations below are exactly the dot-prefixed paths tracked in git, so the
# rule is "repo content is admitted, local state is not" rather than a list
# someone has to remember to extend. tests/governance.test.js asserts every one
# of them exists, and the suite runs inside this image — dropping any would fail
# the harness rather than fail quietly. .keys/ is deliberately NOT re-admitted:
# it is untracked and holds the private signing key.
.*
!.cursorrules
!.dockerignore
!.gitattributes
!.github
!.gitignore
!.gitleaks.toml
!.npmrc
!.nvmrc
!.windsurfrules

# Repo-generated local state that is not dot-prefixed.
refresh-report.json

# Atomic-write orphans. The plain `*.tmp` rule below misses the
# `<name>.tmp.<pid>.<rand>` form the writers actually leave behind when a
# process dies mid-write.
*.tmp.*

# Python tooling artifacts.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
.venv/
venv/

# docker/ and .dockerignore are NOT excluded. Keeping a Dockerfile out of its
# own build context saves a few KB and reads tidy, but both are tracked repo
# content that the governance gate requires to exist — so the image was the one
# place they were missing, and the suite failed in here for a reason that could
# never happen on a runner. Docker reads the Dockerfile through -f regardless of
# the context, so nothing needed the exclusion.
